    },
    "K20": {
        "V0": "The Traditional Folk Music of the Tamil people, who live mainly in the Southeastern tip of India in the state of Tamil Nadu as well as in northern Sri Lanka; a notable population lives in Malaysia & Singapore. Tamil music has existed since ancient times, dating back to the Sangam period (500 BCE - 200 CE), with its ancient form being a precursor to both Tamil folk and Carnatic Classical Music. \n\nFolk music is played in many different contexts, such as during festivals like pongal and during agricultural work, where types of Work Songs are very common, including etra padalgal irrigation songs and temmangu water-lifting dialogues. A specific style of Tamil folk that remains very well known is villu paatu, a style of storytelling interspersed with music named after the villu (bow of a stringed instrument), which may be played between two separate groups in a substyle known as lavani pattu. Musical theatre forms also exist, such as terukkuttu and bommalattam puppet drama. Percussion based ensembles such as Urumi melam and nayyandi melam are also popular.",
        "V1": null
    },
    "K21": {
        "V0": "https://www.indianculture.gov.in/folk-music-life-tamil-nadu\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/3520429?seq=1\nhttp://www.hotelstamilnadu.com/folkmusic.htm\n\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_culture#Music\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Tamil_music\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamils",
        "V1": null
    },
